1. What is Patching?

Patching means installing software updates on the operating system to:

Fix bugs
Fix security vulnerabilities
Improve system stability
Improve performance
Add small improvements

Think of it like updating apps on your mobile phone.

2. Real-Life Example

Suppose your phone has WhatsApp.

One day, WhatsApp releases a new update.

Why?

Fix bugs
Improve security
Add new features

You update WhatsApp.

This is called patching.

Similarly, Oracle releases patches for Solaris.

The administrator installs those patches.

3. Patching Process
Step 1: Check Current Version

The administrator checks the current Solaris version.

Example command:

uname -a

or

pkg info entire

Step 2: Check Available Updates

The administrator checks whether new patches are available.

Example:

pkg update -nv

This only checks what would be updated without actually installing anything.

Step 3: Install the Patch

Install the updates:

pkg update

Solaris downloads and installs the latest patches.

Step 4: Reboot (if required)

Some kernel or system patches require a reboot.

init 6

or

reboot

After reboot, the new patch becomes active.

